'''Teteté''' is an [[extinct language|extinct]] [[Tucanoan]] language that was spoken in [[Ecuador]] close to the Ecuador-Colombia border. It was also formerly spoken in [[Colombia]], but is now [[extinct language|extinct]] in both countries. It was spoken by the [[Indigenous people|indigenous]] [[Tetete people]], who did not survive the twentieth century.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://filebox.vt.edu/users/brmill10/portfolio/research-amazon.html |title=Texaco’s Failed Attempt at Environmental Stewardship in the Ecuadorian Amazon Basin |access-date=6 January 2006 |archive-date=24 December 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141224014531/http://filebox.vt.edu/users/brmill10/portfolio/research-amazon.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Wasserstrom |first=Robert |last2=Reider |first2=Susan |last3=Lara |first3=Rommel |date=1 July 2011 |title=Nobody Knew Their Names: The Black Legend of Tetete Extermination |url=https://read.dukeupress.edu/ethnohistory/article/58/3/421/9014/Nobody-Knew-Their-Names-The-Black-Legend-of-Tetete |journal=Ethnohistory |language=en |volume=58 |issue=3 |pages=421–444 |doi=10.1215/00141801-1263848 |issn=0014-1801|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

Tetete is close to the [[Secoya language]].

